      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="prevention">
        <h2>Prevention: Condition Monitoring and Formulation Controls</h2>
        <p>
          The most cost-effective approach to lubricant oxidation management is not reactive replacement but a proactive condition monitoring programme that detects degradation at the early stage — when corrective action is least disruptive and least expensive — and that implements process controls to slow the oxidation rate from the outset. A well-designed programme integrates oil sampling at defined intervals, a consistent analytical panel, and clearly defined action limits that trigger responses before equipment damage occurs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sampling frequency depends on the severity of the operating environment: highly loaded, high-temperature systems such as gas turbines and large compressors typically require monthly sampling; moderate-duty industrial hydraulic and circulating systems are commonly sampled quarterly. The sampling point matters as much as the interval — samples taken from active circulation lines near the pump suction provide representative fluid in motion; sump-bottom sampling collects settled contaminants and is only useful for sediment analysis. Temperature management is the single most impactful preventive control: reducing bulk oil temperature by 10°C theoretically doubles the oil's remaining service life. Practical temperature reduction measures include cooler maintenance (fouling removal), increased cooling fluid flow rates, addition of supplementary cooling where system loads have increased beyond original design, and ensuring reservoir headspace ventilation prevents thermal stratification. For systems where water ingress is a confirmed contributor to oxidation acceleration, desiccant breathers on reservoir fill points and vent lines eliminate atmospheric moisture ingress — a simple, low-cost intervention with substantial impact on both water content and oxidation rate. The rust preventive oil technology context relevant to corrosion-oxidation interaction is covered in our <a href="/rust-preventive-oils-soft-hard-film" target="_top">rust preventive oils guide</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          A used oil analysis programme without defined action limits is not a condition monitoring programme — it is a data collection exercise. Every analytical parameter in the programme must have a pre-agreed action limit (investigate) and a condemning limit (change oil), documented before sampling begins. Action limits without defined responses provide no protection.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="when-to-escalate">
        <h2>When to Escalate to a Specialist</h2>
        <p>
          Most in-service lubricant oxidation problems can be identified and addressed through standard used oil analysis and systematic maintenance investigation. However, certain patterns indicate that the root cause is beyond the diagnostic capability of routine maintenance programmes and that specialist lubricant engineering support is warranted.
        </p>
        <p>
          Escalation is appropriate when oxidation is progressing at a rate significantly faster than expected for the application type and oil specification — suggesting an unidentified root cause condition, a specification mismatch, or a system design problem. When oxidation-related varnish deposits have caused functional equipment failures such as servo valve stiction, control response degradation, or abnormal pressure drop across filters, the system requires specialist varnish removal and flushing assessment before simply returning to normal operation with fresh oil. Unexplained metal content increases in used oil analysis — particularly elevated copper, lead, or iron — alongside oxidation indicators suggest that oxidation products are directly attacking bearing or system metallurgy, which requires both root cause investigation and assessment of whether bearing or component damage has already occurred. For compressor oils operating in high-discharge-temperature applications, RPVOT values declining faster than expected may indicate a base oil quality issue or an additive interaction problem that requires the oil supplier's technical support alongside specialist independent analysis. Our consulting team provides <a href="/compressor-oils-air-refrigeration-gas" target="_top">compressor oil</a> specification review and used oil analysis interpretation as part of structured root cause investigation engagements.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What are the first signs of lubricant oxidation in service?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">The earliest detectable signs are colour darkening (amber-gold to brown) and a measurable rise in total acid number (TAN) above new-oil baseline — both detectable through used oil analysis before any physical equipment symptom appears. A slight viscosity increase and the first appearance of varnish deposits on hot surfaces follow. Colour change alone is a lagging indicator; by the time the oil looks dark, antioxidant depletion is likely well advanced. Routine used oil analysis with TAN and RPVOT trending provides the only reliable early warning.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why does temperature accelerate lubricant oxidation?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Oxidation rate approximately doubles for every 10°C rise in oil temperature — an Arrhenius relationship. At higher temperatures, oxygen reacts more readily with hydrocarbon chains, peroxy radical formation accelerates, and antioxidant additives are consumed faster. Localised hot spots at bearings, pump pressure zones, and restriction orifices can substantially exceed bulk oil temperature, creating high-rate oxidation sites even when the sump temperature appears acceptable. Temperature management is the single highest-impact preventive lever available for extending oil service life.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What does acid number tell you about oil oxidation?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Total acid number (TAN, mg KOH/g per ASTM D974 or D664) measures the concentration of acidic compounds — oxidation products, antioxidant decomposition products, and their derivatives. A rising TAN relative to new-oil baseline directly indicates oxidative degradation. Condemning limits vary by application: turbine oils typically at 0.5–1.0 mg KOH/g rise above baseline; hydraulic and gear oils have application-specific limits. TAN is most useful when trended over time in a condition monitoring programme, not evaluated as a single data point.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can oxidised oil be rejuvenated, or must it be replaced?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Severely oxidised oil cannot be effectively rejuvenated and must be replaced. Oxidation products — acids, polymeric sludge, varnish precursors — cannot be removed by adding fresh antioxidant; the damaged base oil fractions remain and continue contributing to deposits and corrosion. Make-up oil addition can temporarily dilute oxidation products and replenish some antioxidant capacity but does not reverse damage. Kidney-loop filtration may extend service life for marginally degraded oil but requires continuous condition monitoring to confirm recovery.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How does water contamination accelerate lubricant oxidation?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Water hydrolyses key antioxidant additives (particularly ZDDP) and promotes hydroperoxide formation and stabilisation — both of which accelerate the oxidation chain. Water combined with iron from system metallurgy catalyses Fenton reactions that convert hydroperoxides to highly reactive hydroxyl radicals, dramatically accelerating propagation. Even dissolved water below the saturation threshold accelerates oxidation measurably, making water exclusion and demulsibility control important parameters in systems vulnerable to oxidative degradation.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>When should lubricant oxidation investigation be escalated to a specialist?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Escalate when: oxidation is progressing faster than expected for the application; varnish or sludge deposits have caused functional equipment failures; elevated metal content alongside oxidation indicators suggests corrosive attack on system components; or RPVOT values are declining at a rate inconsistent with the expected antioxidant depletion rate. Specialist investigation uses RPVOT, RULER voltammetry, FTIR, and elemental analysis in combination to diagnose root causes beyond the scope of routine used oil analysis programmes.</div>
        </details>
      </section>

